\paragraph{Method-level interventions.} Three method-level strategies target different aspects of the independence bottleneck on Mistral-7B (Table~\ref{tab:method_interventions}). Independent Constraint Generation (ICG) bypasses trace-constraint coupling by generating constraints directly from the problem statement, producing near-zero average effect ($\Delta = -1.96$\,pp and $+1.47$\,pp across two FOLIO seeds; $-0.50$\,pp on LogiQA; McNemar $p = .206$, n.s.). Contrastive scoring generates pro and con constraints for each candidate but yields $\Delta = -9.31$\,pp on FOLIO ($p < 0.01$) and $-4.50$\,pp on LogiQA ($p = .160$, n.s.), with ${\sim}$21\% degenerate scores on both datasets. Debate adds a proposer-critic-defense chain, producing $\Delta = -18.63$\,pp on FOLIO and $-23.00$\,pp on LogiQA, both statistically significant by McNemar's test ($p < 0.001$ on both datasets). Discordant-pair analysis reveals the asymmetry: on FOLIO, 60 questions flipped from correct to wrong versus only 22 from wrong to correct; on LogiQA, 64 versus 18. The method actively destroys SC's correct predictions rather than introducing balanced noise. Three of five method-dataset conditions reach significance, confirming that degradation is systematic, not stochastic. At the 7B scale, multi-role prompting adds cognitive overhead that degrades both reasoning and extraction quality.

